        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Weight Plates</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these plates. They were chosen from makers' product pages, published
            specifications, warranty terms, recall records and independent coverage, and the claims
            on this page are limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started with the brands most often compared for home gyms — CAP Barbell, REP Fitness,
            Titan Fitness, Yes4All and BalanceFrom — and looked for plates each maker listed on
            19 October 2026 that Garage Gym Reviews or BarBend had described in enough detail to
            cross-check. Weights, hole sizes, tolerances and warranties went on the cards only where
            the maker and a review agreed.
          </p>
          <p>
            That left out two brands. Yes4All and BalanceFrom sell plates mainly through Amazon and
            other marketplaces, and we found no independent review of their plates with figures to
            check. BalanceFrom's listings also disagreed with each other: one "rubber coated" 245 lb set
            we found was described as solid cast iron with a black enamel finish. We would rather leave
            a brand out than card a product whose material we cannot confirm. Titan's grip plates are
            carded on Titan's own figures, labelled as such, because Titan publishes clear
            specifications and the set was in stock.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some figures came off the cards. Garage Gym Reviews' review of REP's Equalizer iron plates
            dates from 2023 and describes a powder-coated finish and a three-year warranty; REP's
            current USA-made Equalizer iron plates have an E-coat finish and a one-year warranty, so
            the card gives REP's terms in the text and leaves the warranty out of the specification
            line. REP's rubber-coated Olympic plates and CAP's 12-sided rubber grip plates are good
            options we could check only against their makers' own pages, so they are mentioned below
            rather than carded.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every brand was checked against the CPSC recall database by brand name in recall titles,
            manufacturer fields and recall descriptions, and the category was searched for weight
            plates and Olympic plates. We found no recalls of iron or coated weight plates by any
            maker. The weight-plate recalls in the database involve adjustable dumbbells whose plates
            can come loose — covered in our
            <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbell guide</a>
            — and a 2021 recall of Impex's OBB-20 Olympic dumbbell handles, whose collars could slip
            and drop the plates. If you plan to load Olympic plates onto dumbbell handles, check the
            handles and collars before the plates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="iron-vs-coated" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Cast Iron, Rubber-Coated, Urethane or Bumper?</h2>
<p>
            Every plate on this page has a cast-iron core. What differs is the outside, and that
            decides noise, floor protection, smell and price.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Plain cast iron</strong> is the cheapest per pound, the thinnest for its weight
              and practically indestructible. It is also the loudest, it chips paint and floors, and
              its painted or enamelled finish can chip and rust over time. Garage Gym Reviews notes
              that CAP's coating in particular chips and scuffs easily.</li>
            <li><strong>Rubber-coated iron</strong> wraps the plate in rubber. It is quieter when plates
              touch each other or the rack, kinder to floors when you set plates down, and slightly
              thicker. Rubber can smell when new.</li>
            <li><strong>Urethane-coated iron</strong> uses a harder, more expensive polymer. It lasts
              longer than rubber, resists tearing and, according to both REP and Garage Gym Reviews,
              has little or no odour — which matters if your gym is in the house.</li>
            <li><strong>Bumper plates</strong> are solid rubber or urethane with a steel insert, built to
              be dropped. They are much thicker and are a different purchase; see our
              <a href="/best-bumper-plates/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">bumper plate guide</a>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The rule that matters: <strong>coated iron plates are not bumper plates</strong>. REP says
            plainly that it does not recommend dropping any iron plate, coated or not, because it can
            damage the bar, the plates and the floor, and Garage Gym Reviews makes the same point about
            the urethane Equalizers. Coating protects against knocks, not drops. If you squat, bench
            and press in a rack and lower your deadlifts under control, iron plates of any kind are
            fine. If you will drop the bar, buy bumpers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="olympic-vs-standard" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Olympic 2-Inch vs Standard 1-Inch Plates</h2>
<p>
            There are two incompatible sizes of plate. <strong>Olympic plates</strong> have a centre
            hole of about 2 inches — REP lists 52 mm on its Old School plates and Titan 50.6 mm on its
            grip plates — to fit the 50 mm rotating sleeves of an Olympic bar. <strong>Standard
            plates</strong> have a 1-inch hole and fit 1-inch "standard" bars and dumbbell handles,
            whose ends usually do not rotate.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a new home gym, buy Olympic. Every barbell in our
            <a href="/best-barbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">barbell guide</a>, every
            plate-loaded machine and every rack's plate storage is built around 2-inch plates, and
            standard bars are generally made for much lighter loads. Standard plates make sense only
            if you already own 1-inch bars or spinlock dumbbell handles. CAP still sells them: on
            19 October 2026 its 1-inch cast-iron plates ranged from $14.99 for six 1.25 lb plates to
            $99.99 for a single 50 lb plate, and a pair of 25s cost $89.99, about $1.80 a pound.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not try to make standard plates work on an Olympic bar. Adapter sleeves exist, but a
            1-inch plate sitting loosely on a 2-inch sleeve is a poor and unsafe fit, and the money is
            better spent replacing the plates. Check, too, that any plate-loaded machine you own takes
            2-inch plates; most do, and our
            <a href="/best-home-gym-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym machine guide</a>
            covers the plate-loaded options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tolerance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Weight Tolerance: 3%, 2% or Calibrated</h2>
<p>
            Cast-iron plates are poured into moulds, so no two come out identical. Makers publish a
            tolerance — how far from its stated weight a plate may be — and the plates here range from
            3% (REP Old School and Equalizer urethane) to 2% (REP's USA-made Equalizer iron). CAP and
            Titan publish no tolerance for these plates.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Tolerance</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">45 lb plate</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">25 lb plate</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Worst case, two 45s</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±3%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±1.35 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±0.75 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.7 lb apart</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±2%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±0.9 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±0.5 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.8 lb apart</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±1%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±0.45 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±0.25 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.9 lb apart</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Those figures are arithmetic on the stated tolerance, not measurements; most plates land
            closer than their tolerance allows. For general strength training, ±3% is fine, and few
            people can feel a pound of difference between the ends of a loaded bar. Tolerance matters
            more if you compete in powerlifting and want training numbers you can trust, if you use
            small plates to make small jumps, or if you load plates onto dumbbell handles, where a
            mismatch is easier to feel. Calibrated steel plates, accurate to a few grams and much
            thinner, exist for powerlifting competition, but they cost far more and are not carded here.
          </p>
          <p>
            A simple check on arrival: weigh each plate on a bathroom or luggage scale and pair the
            closest ones together. It costs nothing and evens out most of the difference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grip-handles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Grip Handles, Lips and Plate Exercises</h2>
<p>
            Grip plates have cut-outs — six on REP's Equalizers — that make them
            easier to carry, load and unload, and to use on their own for plate presses, carries and
            loaded sit-ups. For most home gyms they are worth having, especially on the 25s and 45s,
            which are awkward to pick up off the floor by the rim.
          </p>
          <p>
            Classic plates without handles have a different advantage. REP's Old School plates have a
            single deep lip around the back, which gives your fingers a secure hold on the rim and is
            how plates were designed for decades. Some lifters prefer them for the look and the sound,
            and Garage Gym Reviews notes that their thin profile leaves room for more weight on the bar.
          </p>
          <p>
            The centre hole matters as much as the handles. A machine-drilled hole, like the one on
            REP's USA-made Equalizer iron plates, fits the sleeve closely, so the plates rattle less
            and sit square on the bar. Cast holes on cheaper plates are looser. Neither is a safety
            problem with collars on, but a closer fit is quieter and more pleasant to use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-per-pound" class="scroll-mt-24">
