            figures below are our arithmetic from the prices on each maker's store on 19 October 2026,
            before any shipping charges or discount codes. Where a maker sells only pairs, the 245 lb
            set price is the sum of the pairs that make it up (a pair each of 45, 35, 25, 10, 5 and
            2.5 lb plates).
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Plates</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Bundle priced</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Price</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Per lb</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">CAP Olympic Grip Plates</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">285 lb pack</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$359.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $1.26</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">CAP Olympic Grip Plates</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">one 45 lb plate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$59.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $1.33</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Titan Grip Plates</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">245 lb set (on sale)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$431.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $1.76</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">REP Old School Iron</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">pair of 45s</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$169.98</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $1.89</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">REP Old School Iron</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">245 lb, from pairs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$528.43</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $2.16</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">REP Equalizer Iron (USA)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">245 lb, from pairs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$785.01</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $3.20</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">REP Equalizer Urethane</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">245 lb, from pairs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$845.94</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $3.45</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two patterns are worth knowing before you order. Small plates cost far more per pound than
            big ones: a pair of REP Old School 2.5s is $22.50, or $4.50 a pound, against $1.89 for the
            45s. That is why a set heavy in 45s looks cheap per pound, and why the CAP 285 lb pack beats
            CAP's own singles. And makers' own set prices may differ from the sum of the pairs — REP
            lists six set sizes for each plate, from 175 to 425 lb — so price the set and the pairs
            before you choose.
          </p>
          <p>
            Shipping is the hidden cost. Plates are heavy, and a "cheap" set with a large freight
            charge can cost more than a dearer one shipped free. Check the delivered price, and whether
            a local store can sell you the same plates without freight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. How Much Weight Should You Buy?</h2>
<p>
            Buy for the heaviest lift you will do in the next year, usually the deadlift, plus the
            small plates you need to progress. A common starting point is a 245 lb set — a pair each of
            45, 35, 25, 10, 5 and 2.5 lb plates — which with a 45 lb bar loads up to 290 lb. One pair
            of each leaves a few gaps (you cannot make 85 lb, for example); add a second pair of 10s
            and every 5 lb step from 50 to 310 lb is covered. A 175 lb set without the 35s is enough for many beginners for a few
            months, and a second pair of 45s is the usual first addition.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you lift with a partner of very different strength, extra small plates matter more than
            extra 45s, because you will be changing the bar constantly. If you also use dumbbell
            handles or a plate-loaded machine, count the plates those need at the same time as the bar.
          </p>
          <p>
            Store plates on a tree or on your rack's plate horns, low and evenly loaded, not stacked
            on the floor or leaning against a wall. Our
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat rack guide</a>
            explains why heavy storage belongs low on the rack, our
            <a href="/best-weight-benches/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight bench guide</a>
            covers the bench you will press from, and our
            <a href="/best-home-gym-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym accessories guide</a>
            covers plate trees and collars.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, protect the floor. Iron plates set down on concrete chip both the plates and the
            slab; on wood, tile or vinyl they dent and crack. Even if you never drop the bar, a layer of
            <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">rubber gym flooring</a>
            under the lifting area is worth having, and a
            <a href="/best-lifting-platforms/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifting platform</a>
            is the answer for heavy deadlifts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the best weight plates for a home gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, Olympic 2-inch plates with grip handles from a maker that publishes a tolerance. REP's urethane-coated Equalizers are the best overall if you can pay about $3.45 a pound for a set; REP's Old School iron plates are the best value at about $2.16; CAP's grip plates are the cheapest at about $1.26.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are rubber-coated plates better than cast iron?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are quieter and kinder to floors and racks, and less likely to chip, but they cost more and can smell when new. Cast iron is cheaper, thinner and lasts indefinitely. Neither should be dropped. Choose coated plates for a gym in the house or a shared space, and iron for a garage on a budget.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between Olympic and standard weight plates?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Olympic plates have a centre hole of about 2 inches and fit Olympic bars with 50 mm rotating sleeves. Standard plates have a 1-inch hole and fit 1-inch bars and spinlock handles. They are not interchangeable. For a new home gym, buy Olympic plates, because racks, bars and plate-loaded machines are built around them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you drop rubber-coated weight plates?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Rubber- and urethane-coated plates are still iron plates, and REP says it does not recommend dropping any iron plate because it can damage the bar, the plates and the floor. If you plan to drop the bar in cleans, snatches or heavy deadlifts, buy bumper plates, which are made for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should weight plates cost per pound?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 19 October 2026, the plates we checked ranged from about $1.26 a pound for CAP's cast-iron grip plates in a large pack to about $3.45 for REP's urethane-coated plates in a 245 lb set. Small plates cost much more per pound than 45s, and shipping can change the comparison.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What weight plates should a beginner buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 245 lb set — a pair each of 45, 35, 25, 10, 5 and 2.5 lb plates — loads a 45 lb bar up to 290 lb and covers most beginners for a long time; a second pair of 10s fills the gaps between 5 lb steps. A 175 lb set without the 35s is a cheaper start; add a second pair of 45s when your deadlift needs it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying Weight Plates</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these is easy to avoid before you order and expensive to correct afterwards.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying standard plates for an Olympic bar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Cheap 1-inch standard plates will not fit a 2-inch Olympic sleeve properly, and adapters are a poor fix. Check the centre hole — about 2 inches for Olympic plates — before ordering, especially when buying used or in a bundle.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating coated plates as bumpers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Rubber and urethane coatings protect against knocks, not drops. Dropping coated iron plates can crack them, bend the bar and damage the floor. If your training includes drops, buy bumper plates for those lifts.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing prices by the plate, not the pound</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A set full of small plates looks cheap but costs more per pound, and freight can turn the cheapest set into the dearest. Work out the delivered price per pound for the plates you actually need before you compare.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping collars and uneven loading</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Plates slide on a moving bar, and one side loaded more than the other can tip a bar off its J-cups. Load and unload both sides alternately, use collars on every set, and keep heavy plates low on the rack's storage.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

